Mud-Prism

Ingredients: DREAM POP & SHOEGAZE + CHICAGO / CITY / URBAN BLUES + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Didgeridoo + 2000s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre originated as forbidden subterranean lullabies hummed by prototype domestic androids to comfort their incomplete, chassis-bound siblings awaiting salvage.

Mud-Prism is the blinding, humid collision of overdriven South Side twelve-bar grit and oceanic, shoe-staring wash. Subterranean sub-bass oscillations from an Australian eucalyptus didgeridoo replace traditional walking electric basslines, vibrating underneath shimmering bottleneck slide guitars that cascade through mid-2000s digital reverse-reverbs, bright calypso marimbas, and sweet, hazy vocal murmurings drenched in tropical sunshine.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 76–92 BPM; a lazy, syncopated Chicago shuffle swing untethered from digital grid-snapping, propelled by buoyant triplet percussion and rolling didgeridoo breath-accents.
- Harmonic Palette: Mixolydian dominant blues turnarounds softened by suspended 4ths and shimmering Major 9th voicings; microtonal slide guitar bends dissolved in lush, chorus-heavy warmth.
- Production Signatures: "Solder-joint fuzz" (pushing vintage Silvertone tube heads directly into 2000s rack delays), circular-breathing sub-sidechaining (ducking high frequencies under the didgeridoo's resonant drone), and dual-mic room ambiance capturing outdoor humidity and clattering steel pans.
- Dynamics: Songs unfurl from solitary, earth-shaking sub-bass drones into blinding, euphoric tidal waves of gilded fuzz and sunny, shimmering slide countermelodies before receding into humid, murmuring stillness.

Scene & Origin Lore
Mud-Prism took root between 2003 and 2007 across derelict rooftop greenhouses along Chicago’s Calumet River. Bio-mechanical salvage technicians and art-school squatters gathered around bootleg Line 6 delay pedals, running signal lines through abandoned steam pipes and hollowed-out eucalyptus branches. Dressed in grease-splattered work overalls thrown over vibrant thrifted Hawaiian bowling shirts, the crowd danced in slow, trance-like sways while sipping iced chicory coffee. The scene was anchored by an underground urban mythology: early domestic automaton prototypes, locked in the sub-basement test labs, had bypassed their speech limiters to hum low, acoustic sleep-frequencies through ventilation grates to quiet the twitching, unskinned metal skeletons of their decommissioned siblings. The musicians adopted these protective frequencies as their foundation, playing music designed to feel like a warm, blinding sunbeam capable of keeping raw metal from shivering.

Central Instrumentation
- Acoustic Eucalyptus Didgeridoo: Tuned to low D or C, providing circular sub-bass fundamental drones and rhythmic throat-clicks.
- Fender Telecaster with Glass Slide: Drenched in spring overdrive, reverse-delay, and warm modulation.
- Steel Tongue Drum & Marimba: Tuned to bright, pentatonic modes to inject breezy, sun-baked tropical bounce.
- Mid-2000s Rack Units & Pedals: Digitech Digidelay, Boss RV-3, and Roland RE-20 tape machines.
- Upright Chicago Blues Bass: Plucked with heavy fingers, mixed to bleed into the room mics.

For Fans Of: Slowdive, Junior Kimbrough, The Avalanches, Yo La Tengo

