- Story & Context: On a sweltering July evening in 2004 inside an abandoned Pullman switching yard, sound scavenger Ellis Vance plugged an uncalibrated android voice-chip directly into Bo’s didgeridoo during a live set, causing the room's electrical junction boxes to hum in resonant sympathy. The sudden surge triggered a swarm of shoreline cicadas to coat the greenhouse windows, their wings vibrating against the glass as the band locked into an unyielding delta groove that smelled of scorched solder and tropical mango syrup. The police raided the perimeter thirty minutes later, but the dancing crowd linked arms and drowned out the sirens by roaring the track's climactic phrase, "we wrap your copper bones in daylight," into the heavy summer haze.
